{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Patient-related outcome measures (PROM) such as quality of life (QoL) after hernia repair are important to monitor, since QoL is an important indication of a hernia repair. Carolinas Comfort Scale (CCS) is a hernia-specific questionnaire regarding health-related QoL after a mesh hernia repair. The primary objective of this study was to monitor changes in health-related QoL over time after different mesh hernia repair procedures using the CCS. METHODS: We performed a prospective study with an exploratory, intraobserver design enrolling consecutive patients who were to undergo elective mesh hernia repair. Four different hernia repair techniques were included and analyzed specifically for each surgical group. Patients scored themselves using CCS preoperatively and at several fixed times postoperatively. Since no gold standard exists for either hernia-specific QoL questionnaires or change of scores on PROMs as a function of time after hernia repair, we compared the CCS scores with the visual analog scale (VAS) scores reflecting the subdomains of the CCS. RESULTS: A total of 166 patients completed the study. CCS scores for QoL, pain, sensation of mesh, and activity limitations changed significantly with time during the 90-day study period. Furthermore, CCS and VAS showed significant agreement and correlation (ρ = 0.52-0.82, P < 0.001). CONCLUSIONS: The present study is the first to demonstrate significant changes over time in health-related QoL using the CCS after four different types of hernia repair with mesh.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Christoffersen, Mette W","Rosenberg, Jacob","Jørgensen, Lars Nannestad","Bytzer, Peter","Bisgaard, Thue"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-494093"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"health related quality of life scores changes significantly within the first three months after hernia mesh repair","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00268-013-2411-0","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-494093","identifier":[{"id":"84905097888","type":"scopus"},{"id":"24366274","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-a884b5a3-821f-44dd-9249-dbbb9a872c2b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D1E73701BE84C605C9902A2137E24D0A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00268-013-2411-0","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Mette W","key":"christoffersenpau","last":"Christoffersen","name":"Christoffersen, Mette W","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jacob","key":"rosenbergpau","last":"Rosenberg","locid":"00b94c43-5ffb-4e1e-ac75-320aa0c79745","name":"Rosenberg, Jacob","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lars Nannestad","key":"jorgensenpau","last":"Jorgensen","locid":"ae2a79a1-5374-4e92-84c9-3af55ef5712e","name":"Jørgensen, Lars Nannestad","name_org":"Jorgensen, Lars Nannestad","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Peter","key":"bytzerpau","last":"Bytzer","locid":"5567dcaa-8b77-40ff-ba72-b3879e0beefd","name":"Bytzer, Peter","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thue","key":"bisgaardpau","last":"Bisgaard","locid":"257c59f7-fb76-4527-a919-eef7f4459809","name":"Bisgaard, Thue","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24366274"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00268-013-2411-0","issn":["03642313","14322323"],"issn_org":["03642313"],"issue":"7","journal":"World Journal of Surgery","pages":"1852-1859","status":"p","title":"World Journal of Surgery","volume":"38"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84905097888"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-a884b5a3-821f-44dd-9249-dbbb9a872c2b"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Health-related Quality of Life Scores Changes Significantly within the First Three Months After Hernia Mesh Repair","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-494093","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"The Göttingen minipig model of obesity is used in pre-clinical research to predict clinical outcome of new treatments for metabolic diseases. However, treatment effects often remain unnoticed when using single parameter statistical comparisons due to the small numbers of animals giving rise to large variation and insufficient statistical power. The purpose of this study was to perform a correlation matrix analysis of multiple multi-scale parameters describing co-segregation of traits in order to identify differences between lean and obese minipigs. More than 40 parameters, ranging from physical, cardiovascular, inflammatory and metabolic markers were measured in lean and obese animals. Correlation matrix analysis was performed using permutation test and bootstrapping at different levels of significance. Single parameter comparisons yielded significant differences between lean and obese animals mainly for known physical traits. On the other hand, functional network analysis revealed new co-segregations, particularly in the domain of inflammatory and oxidative stress markers in the obese animals that were not present in the lean. Functional networks of lean or obese minipigs could be utilised to assess drug effects and predict changes in parameters with a certain degree of precision, on the basis of the networks confidence intervals. Comparison of functional networks in minipigs with those of human clinical data may be used to identify common parameters or co-segregations related to obesity between animal models and man.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Boonen, Harrie C. M.","Moesgaard, Sophia G.","Birck, Malene M.","Christoffersen, Berit Ø.","Cirera, Susanna","Heegaard, Peter M. H.","Højbøge, Tina Rødgaard","Jensen, Lars Juhl","Mortensen, Alan","Olsen, Lisbeth Høier","Sheykhzade, Majid","Tang, Jiaowei","Lykkesfeldt, Jens"],"cuid":["S02-086.61-034142"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-NOVO"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOVO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-NP-NOVO","DI-PR-NOVO","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"functional network analysis of obese and lean göttingen minipigs elucidates changes in oxidative and inflammatory networks in obese pigs","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/131950294/10.1007_s00424_014_1486_2.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-086.61-034142","identifier":[{"id":"24590510","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84912013464","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-44c479c6-851f-4bc0-aad9-7ae23763cc9d","ku-696f26f6-1277-42e2-8074-c7405b05b793"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-2CDC31EB87B875622679DE0B5EFAB975"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/131950294/10.1007_s00424_014_1486_2.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00424-014-1486-2","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00424-014-1486-2","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Drug Design and Pharmacology, Experimental Pharmacology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{