{"abstract":"Oxidation of LDL plays a key role in the development of atherosclerosis. HDL may, in part, protect against atherosclerosis by inhibiting LDL oxidation. Overexpression of HDL-associated apolipoprotein M (apoM) protects mice against atherosclerosis through a not yet clarified mechanism. Being a lipocalin, apoM contains a binding pocket for small lipophilic molecules. Here, we report that apoM likely serves as an antioxidant in HDL by binding oxidized phospholipids, thus enhancing the antioxidant potential of HDL.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Elsøe, Sara","Ahnström, Josefin","Christoffersen, Christina","Hoofnagle, Andrew N","Plomgaard, Peter","Heinecke, Jay W","Binder, Christoph J","Björkbacka, Harry","Dahlbäck, Björn","Nielsen, Lars Bo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-518086"],"filters":{"added":"2022-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Amidines","Animals","Antioxidants","Apolipoproteins","Atherosclerosis","Binding Sites","Cholesterol Dietary","Disease Models Animal","Humans","Lipocalins","Lipoproteins HDL","Lipoproteins LDL","Mice","Mice Inbred C57BL","Mice Knockout","Mice Transgenic","Myristic acid","Oxidants","Oxidation Reduction","Phospholipids","Receptors LDL","Recombinant Proteins","Thiobarbituric acid reactive substances","Time Factors"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"apolipoprotein m binds oxidized phospholipids and increases the antioxidant effect of hdl","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.atherosclerosis.2011.11.031","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-518086","identifier":[{"id":"22204862","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84857029613","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-d0a3aeb4-8c94-41d2-ba72-93510fff8fba"],"keyword"